From f6f1d26617e05731953ad59fe502927c99a1ed86 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abhay-byte <zenithblue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 07:41:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] panvk: device-wide caches for command buffer CS,
 descriptor and TLS slabs

Rationale: perf review 2026-10-26 part B, finding B1. Every VkCommandPool
kept its own free lists of 64 KiB slabs, trimmed by csf-v11 226/231 to the
largest count one of its resets got back in the last one or two windows of
64 resets, plus 25 %. DXVK records one command list at a time but recycles
about 20 pools and resets each with flags = 0 about every 2 s at
Fallout 4's 11 submits/s, so a pool's window is about 2 minutes and the
footprint was the sum of all per-pool peaks: cmd_cs + cmd_desc were
125-155 MiB (2000-2480 slabs) in 112, most of them idle.

Now each kind of command buffer slab (CS, descriptor, TLS) has one cache
for the whole device. After a flags = 0 vkResetCommandPool the pool keeps
2 free slabs of each cached kind for its next recording and hands the
rest to the caches; vkDestroyCommandPool hands them all. A recording takes
from its pool's free list first, then from the cache (one uncontended
mutex per 64 KiB slab), then allocates. The cache gives out its newest
slab and, at the first hand-off after each window of
PANVK_CMD_SLAB_CACHE_MS (default 2000 ms), frees its oldest slabs down to
a quarter of the window's swing (at least 2): with takes from the tail,
the slabs below the window's lowest count were not used in the whole
window. A spike is freed one to two windows after it ends, whatever the
number of pools. csf-v11 231 still trims the other free lists (the big
TLS BOs, JM). RELEASE_RESOURCES resets and vkTrimCommandPool free the
pool's own free lists as before; the caches keep theirs.

Cache coherency is that of reuse within a pool: slabs enter a cache only
when the command buffers that used them are reset or freed (not pending),
CPU writes are flushed at EndCommandBuffer as before, and the kind keeps
the BO flags.

PANVK_DEBUG=mem: the report line gains "slab_cache=<MiB>/<slabs>
hits=<taken> freed=<slabs> <MiB>"; the cached slabs are still counted in
cmd_cs / cmd_desc / cmd_tls.

Knob: PANVK_CMD_SLAB_CACHE (default 1, read once at device creation);
0 = 114 (per-pool free lists, 231 trim). PANVK_CMD_SLAB_CACHE_MS (default
2000, 100-600000) sets the window. Arch: v10+ (CSF); JM keeps its
per-pool lists.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
